      "updated": 1789376981600,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\"Most (I assume all) data were first concatenated into 30-second segments, primarily preserving the\nsame speaker, then inferred together\" — so the same speaker-grouped, ~30s-packing approach as\nconcatenate_dataset, applied as the default but not to literally every sample (\"most,\" not \"all\").\nOnly 20% of segments were trained with previous context (\"since we don't expect the 2-layer decoder\nto excel at this task\") — i.e. condition_on_prev was used sparingly, not everywhere, because their\ndistilled student has a very shallow decoder (2 layers) that they judged wouldn't benefit much from\nlong-context conditioning anyway.\n50% were trained with timestamps — a separate axis of variation, mixed independently of the\nconcatenation/context choices.\nSources were short-utterance corpora (Common Voice, MLS, VoxPopuli, MTedX, YODAS) — exactly the\n\"many short clips\" case where vLLM's chunking doesn't help, confirming concatenation was doing real\nwork here, not something rendered redundant by the serving backend.\nThey also ran WER-threshold filtering (dropped anything >20% WER against... likely the teacher's own\nre-scoring or reference), cutting 11,000h down to ~6,500h — the \"separate filtering step\" I mentioned\nearlier isn't optional in practice; it's clearly part of getting a usable distillation dataset.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\"Most (I assume all) data were first concatenated into 30-second segments, primarily preserving the same speaker, then inferred together\" — so the same speaker-grouped, ~30s-packing approach as concatenate_dataset, applied as the default but not to literally every sample (\"most,\" not \"all\").\nOnly 20% of segments were trained with previous context (\"since we don't expect the 2-layer decoder to excel at this task\") — i.e. condition_on_prev was used sparingly, not everywhere, because their distilled student has a very shallow decoder (2 layers) that they judged wouldn't benefit much from long-context conditioning anyway.\n50% were trained with timestamps — a separate axis of variation, mixed independently of the concatenation/context choices.\nSources were short-utterance corpora (Common Voice, MLS, VoxPopuli, MTedX, YODAS) — exactly the \"many short clips\" case where vLLM's chunking doesn't help, confirming concatenation was doing real work here, not something rendered redundant by the serving backend.\nThey also ran WER-threshold filtering (dropped anything >20% WER against... likely the teacher's own re-scoring or reference), cutting 11,000h down to ~6,500h — the \"separate filtering step\" I mentioned earlier isn't optional in practice; it's clearly part of getting a usable distillation dataset.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
