    {
      "id": "RhM8N34USMVGpSTfTEAYx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2362.7184300524577,
      "y": 20.05974169629826,
      "width": 934.6541748046875,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "Os8NnrRhe_GLidOIpCMaV",
        "MMr3vdJwTeBZkOcH537uJ",
        "q6AxoQdXmcfwZw3AFDlW4",
        "0SdD_6dTTZkiDfCbdeSGQ"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 386069996,
      "version": 1774,
      "versionNonce": 918733036,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789376975116,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Many insects have a larval form that is optimized for extracting energy and nutrients from the\nenvironment and a completely different adult form that is optimized for the very different\nrequirements of traveling and reproduction.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn large-scale machine learning, we typically use very similar models for the training stage and\nthe deployment stage despite their very different requirements: For tasks\nlike speech and object recognition, training must extract structure from very large, highly\nredundant datasets but it does not need to operate in real time and it can use a huge amount\nof computation.\nDeployment to a large number of users, however, has much more stringent requirements on\nlatency and computational resources.\n\nThe cumbersome model could be an ensemble of separately trained models or a single very large\nmodel. Once the cumbersome model has been trained, we can then use a different kind of\ntraining, which we call “distillation” to transfer the knowledge from the cumbersome model to a\nsmall model that is more suitable for deployment.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Many insects have a larval form that is optimized for extracting energy and nutrients from the environment and a completely different adult form that is optimized for the very different requirements of traveling and reproduction.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn large-scale machine learning, we typically use very similar models for the training stage and the deployment stage despite their very different requirements: For tasks\nlike speech and object recognition, training must extract structure from very large, highly redundant datasets but it does not need to operate in real time and it can use a huge amount of computation.\nDeployment to a large number of users, however, has much more stringent requirements on latency and computational resources.\n\nThe cumbersome model could be an ensemble of separately trained models or a single very large model. Once the cumbersome model has been trained, we can then use a different kind of training, which we call “distillation” to transfer the knowledge from the cumbersome model to a small model that is more suitable for deployment.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "35Qwyzk1E4zZbZPTACrel",
