    {
      "id": "pwndAQuKJeQ0B6iajaloN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3789.4176869834982,
      "y": -83.39532473675399,
      "width": 934.6541748046875,
      "height": 2325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "381xyEPADrh4135Jy-yhk",
        "Gjsc0VGf7v_9_ZS-0NPAj",
        "9kIfEsLQk2sTJthq2nTJb"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b322",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1784676436,
      "version": 2162,
      "versionNonce": 1093970747,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "lLScMsdW3pLe3ISt4KNoR",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1789416637429,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Whisper is a family of multilingual speech recognition models introduced by OpenAI.\n\nThe paper continue on the research from Facebook AI on Wav2Vec:\n    1. learn powerful representations from speech audio alone (53k hours)\n    2.  followed by fine-tuning on transcribed speech\n\nSince these methods learn directly from raw audio without the need for human labels, they can\nproductively use large datasets of unlabeled speech and have been quickly scaled up to\n1,000,000 hours of training data.\n\nProblem: These pre-trained audio encoders learn high-quality representations of speech, but\nlack an equivalently performant decoder mapping those representations to usable outputs.\nThey still need a finetuning stage in order to actually perform a task such as speech\nrecognition!\n\nThere is an additional risk with requiring fine-tuning. Machine learning tend to OVERFIT! They\nare exceedingly adept at finding patterns within a training dataset which boost performance\non held-out data from the same dataset. However, some of these patterns are brittle and\nspurious and don’t generalize to other datasets and distributions.\n\nThe goal: create a speech recognition system should be to work reliably “out of the box\"\nwithout requiring supervised fine-tuning of a decoder for every deployment distribution.\n\nWhisper relies on a well-established Transformer encoder–decoder architecture, adapting it to\nprocess audio instead of text as its input.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWhisper operates on audio windows of approximately 30 seconds. During training, longer\nrecordings are divided into 30-second segments together with the corresponding portion of\nthe transcript. This also means that Whisper cannot directly process an arbitrarily long\nrecording in a single forward pass. More on this later\n\nOnce the audio representation has been processed by the encoder, the Transformer decoder\ngenerates text autoregressively, one token at a time. As in other encoder–decoder\nTransformers, the decoder contains both self-attention and cross-attention. Self-attention\nallows the decoder to consider the text tokens it has already generated, while cross-\nattention allows it to attend to the representations produced by the audio encoder.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOpenAI released the original Whisper family in five main model sizes: Tiny, Base, Small, Medium,\nand Large",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Whisper is a family of multilingual speech recognition models introduced by OpenAI.\n\nThe paper continue on the research from Facebook AI on Wav2Vec:\n    1. learn powerful representations from speech audio alone (53k hours)\n    2.  followed by fine-tuning on transcribed speech\n\nSince these methods learn directly from raw audio without the need for human labels, they can productively use large datasets of unlabeled speech and have been quickly scaled up to 1,000,000 hours of training data.\n\nProblem: These pre-trained audio encoders learn high-quality representations of speech, but lack an equivalently performant decoder mapping those representations to usable outputs. They still need a finetuning stage in order to actually perform a task such as speech recognition!\n\nThere is an additional risk with requiring fine-tuning. Machine learning tend to OVERFIT! They are exceedingly adept at finding patterns within a training dataset which boost performance on held-out data from the same dataset. However, some of these patterns are brittle and spurious and don’t generalize to other datasets and distributions.\n\nThe goal: create a speech recognition system should be to work reliably “out of the box\" without requiring supervised fine-tuning of a decoder for every deployment distribution.\n\nWhisper relies on a well-established Transformer encoder–decoder architecture, adapting it to process audio instead of text as its input.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWhisper operates on audio windows of approximately 30 seconds. During training, longer recordings are divided into 30-second segments together with the corresponding portion of the transcript. This also means that Whisper cannot directly process an arbitrarily long recording in a single forward pass. More on this later\n\nOnce the audio representation has been processed by the encoder, the Transformer decoder generates text autoregressively, one token at a time. As in other encoder–decoder Transformers, the decoder contains both self-attention and cross-attention. Self-attention allows the decoder to consider the text tokens it has already generated, while cross-attention allows it to attend to the representations produced by the audio encoder.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOpenAI released the original Whisper family in five main model sizes: Tiny, Base, Small, Medium, and Large",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
