    {
      "id": "yzNwwu5tY3p1OfgODjhiW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3841.3785128808954,
      "y": 5306.6758574820515,
      "width": 934.6541748046875,
      "height": 1425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "eaDisF7f1k5zWSlLtnrSs",
        "YO9XnFvNolQnTqyMXI5WO",
        "8KTg5dKFjXDVGymISbyT1",
        "nozNyMZBQfV2_nm8emOiJ",
        "QSMYwKMSWiuONYgVkO0K1",
        "9kIfEsLQk2sTJthq2nTJb"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b33",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1140063980,
      "version": 2074,
      "versionNonce": 1851348283,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "L1GiawMpZaLai8n0fCOI_",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1789416637430,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Whisper models are trained on 30-second audio chunks and cannot consume longer audio inputs\nat once.\n\nFor academic datasets is not a problem but real-world applications yes!\n\nThe key idea is that Whisper does not simply split a long recording into fixed, independent 30-\nsecond chunks. Instead, it uses a moving 30-second window whose next position is determined\nby the timestamps predicted by the model. \n\nSuppose we have a 10-minute recording. Whisper starts with the first 30 seconds:\n\n                Audio\n                0s ───────────────────────────────────────────── 600s\n                \n                                     [──────── 30 s window ────────]\n                                     0s                              30s\n\nIt transcribes that window while also generating timestamp tokens around the transcription\nsegments, for example:\n\n                                        <0.00> Hello and welcome ...\n                                        <6.20> Today we are going to ...\n                                        <17.80> Let's start with ...\n                                        <27.40> ...\n\nWhisper looks at the timestamps of the successfully decoded segments and uses them to\ndecide how far to advance the audio window. So instead of blindly jumping from 0–30 s to\n30–60 s, it might determine that the last reliable segment ended at 27.4 seconds and\ncontinue from there:\n\n                                Window 1\n                                0 ───────────────── 30\n                                [====================]\n                                                     ↑\n                                                 27.4 s\n                                \n                                Window 2\n                                                    27.4 ───────────────── 57.4\n                                                    [========================]\n\nThere is a problem, however: an error in one window can propagate to subsequent windows. If\nWhisper predicts a bad timestamp, the next 30-second window may start at the wrong\nlocation. The paper therefore adds several decoding heuristics specifically for long-form\ntranscription:\n\n1. Whisper initially uses beam search with 5 beam and temperature fallback: if the average log\nprobability is below −1 or the text is excessively repetitive, the model retries with progressively\nhigher temperatures. The idea is that if deterministic decoding gets stuck in a bad sequence,\nintroducing more sampling diversity may allow the model to escape repetitive loops (a well known\nproblem in whisper).\n2. feed the text from the previous window back into the decoder as context. This helps\nmaintain continuity across windows. The paper applies this previous-text conditioning when the\ndecoding temperature is below 0.5.\n3. Finally, the algorithm has safeguards for silence. A high <|nospeech|> probability by itself\nwas not sufficiently reliable, so Whisper combines a no-speech probability threshold of 0.6 with\nan average log-probability threshold of −1 to decide when a window should be treated as\ncontaining no useful speech.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Whisper models are trained on 30-second audio chunks and cannot consume longer audio inputs at once.\n\nFor academic datasets is not a problem but real-world applications yes!\n\nThe key idea is that Whisper does not simply split a long recording into fixed, independent 30-second chunks. Instead, it uses a moving 30-second window whose next position is determined by the timestamps predicted by the model. \n\nSuppose we have a 10-minute recording. Whisper starts with the first 30 seconds:\n\n                Audio\n                0s ───────────────────────────────────────────── 600s\n                \n                                     [──────── 30 s window ────────]\n                                     0s                              30s\n\nIt transcribes that window while also generating timestamp tokens around the transcription segments, for example:\n\n                                        <0.00> Hello and welcome ...\n                                        <6.20> Today we are going to ...\n                                        <17.80> Let's start with ...\n                                        <27.40> ...\n\nWhisper looks at the timestamps of the successfully decoded segments and uses them to decide how far to advance the audio window. So instead of blindly jumping from 0–30 s to 30–60 s, it might determine that the last reliable segment ended at 27.4 seconds and continue from there:\n\n                                Window 1\n                                0 ───────────────── 30\n                                [====================]\n                                                     ↑\n                                                 27.4 s\n                                \n                                Window 2\n                                                    27.4 ───────────────── 57.4\n                                                    [========================]\n\nThere is a problem, however: an error in one window can propagate to subsequent windows. If Whisper predicts a bad timestamp, the next 30-second window may start at the wrong location. The paper therefore adds several decoding heuristics specifically for long-form transcription:\n\n1. Whisper initially uses beam search with 5 beam and temperature fallback: if the average log probability is below −1 or the text is excessively repetitive, the model retries with progressively higher temperatures. The idea is that if deterministic decoding gets stuck in a bad sequence, introducing more sampling diversity may allow the model to escape repetitive loops (a well known problem in whisper).\n2. feed the text from the previous window back into the decoder as context. This helps maintain continuity across windows. The paper applies this previous-text conditioning when the decoding temperature is below 0.5.\n3. Finally, the algorithm has safeguards for silence. A high <|nospeech|> probability by itself was not sufficiently reliable, so Whisper combines a no-speech probability threshold of 0.6 with an average log-probability threshold of −1 to decide when a window should be treated as containing no useful speech.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
