      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Auto-regressive language generation is based on the assumption that the\nprobability distribution of a word sequence can be decomposed into the\nproduct of conditional next word distributions:\n\n\n\n\n\nGreedy search is the simplest decoding strategy. It selects the next most\nlikely token at each step. Starting from the word \"The\", the algorithm\ngreedily chooses the next word of highest probability \"nice\" and so on, so\nthat the final generated word sequence is (\"The\",\"nice\",\"woman\") having an\noverall probability of 0.5×0.4=0.2 .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis makes greedy decoding very fast, but it is locally optimal rather than\nglobally optimal: the token that looks best now may lead to a worse overall\ntranscription. Once an incorrect token is chosen, the model cannot go back\nand reconsider alternative sequences. \nThe word \"has\" with its high conditional probability of 0.9 is hidden behind\nthe word \"dog\", which has only the second-highest conditional probability, so\nthat greedy search misses the word sequence \"The\",\"dog\",\"has\" .\n\nBeam search reduces the risk of missing hidden high probability word\nsequences by keeping the most likely num_beams of hypotheses at each time\nstep and eventually choosing the hypothesis that has the overall highest\nprobability. Let's illustrate with num_beams=2:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAt time step 1, besides the most likely hypothesis (\"The\",\"nice\"), beam search\nalso keeps track of the second most likely one (\"The\",\"dog\"). At time step 2,\nbeam search finds that the word sequence (\"The\",\"dog\",\"has\"), has with 0.36\na higher probability than (\"The\",\"nice\",\"woman\"), which has 0.2",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Auto-regressive language generation is based on the assumption that the probability distribution of a word sequence can be decomposed into the product of conditional next word distributions:\n\n\n\n\n\nGreedy search is the simplest decoding strategy. It selects the next most likely token at each step. Starting from the word \"The\", the algorithm greedily chooses the next word of highest probability \"nice\" and so on, so that the final generated word sequence is (\"The\",\"nice\",\"woman\") having an overall probability of 0.5×0.4=0.2 .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis makes greedy decoding very fast, but it is locally optimal rather than globally optimal: the token that looks best now may lead to a worse overall transcription. Once an incorrect token is chosen, the model cannot go back and reconsider alternative sequences. \nThe word \"has\" with its high conditional probability of 0.9 is hidden behind the word \"dog\", which has only the second-highest conditional probability, so that greedy search misses the word sequence \"The\",\"dog\",\"has\" .\n\nBeam search reduces the risk of missing hidden high probability word sequences by keeping the most likely num_beams of hypotheses at each time step and eventually choosing the hypothesis that has the overall highest probability. Let's illustrate with num_beams=2:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAt time step 1, besides the most likely hypothesis (\"The\",\"nice\"), beam search also keeps track of the second most likely one (\"The\",\"dog\"). At time step 2, beam search finds that the word sequence (\"The\",\"dog\",\"has\"), has with 0.36 a higher probability than (\"The\",\"nice\",\"woman\"), which has 0.2",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
