    {
      "id": "5X9xQhYg1t3cGGsSJuMkt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3829.5783055441457,
      "y": 6961.218510934832,
      "width": 934.6541748046875,
      "height": 2150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "fg_anB-z1cUVN-g-haTYP",
        "G-saU1RwhTnVeYzdW1hFU",
        "ls-pOwxfq8y_G48cWFoe1",
        "9kIfEsLQk2sTJthq2nTJb"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b33V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 590820692,
      "version": 1676,
      "versionNonce": 1578440571,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789416637430,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "English Speech Recognition\n\nAlthough both models perform within 0.1% of each other on LibriSpeech, a zero-shot Whisper\nmodel performs much better on other datasets than expected for its LibriSpeech performance\nand makes 55.2% less errors on average.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nMulti-lingual Speech Recognition and Translation\nWe find a strong squared correlation coefficient of 0.83 between the log of the word error\nrate and the log of the amount of training data per language. We also observed that many of\nthe largest outliers in terms of worse than expected performance according to this trend are\nlanguages that have unique scripts and are more distantly related to the Indo-European\nlanguages making up the majority of the training dataset such as Hebrew (HE), Telugu (TE),\nChinese (ZH), and Korean (KO). These differences could be due to a lack of transfer due to\nlinguistic distance.\n\nFor translation the same correlation emerged, moreover since Whisper evaluation is zero-shot, it\ndoes particularly well on the lowest resource in CoVoST2\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nLanguage Identification\nThe zero-shot performance of Whisper is not competitive with prior supervised work here and\nunderperforms the supervised SOTA by 13.6%.\n\nLong-form Transcription\nWe compare the performance with open-source models as well as 4 commercial ASR services. The\nresults show that Whisper performs better than the compared models on most datasets\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nComparison with Human Performance\nTo quantify how close Whisper’s performance is to the human performance, they selected 25\nrecordings and compared transcripts produced by professional transcribers. These results\nindicate that Whisper’s English ASR performance is not perfect but very close to human-level\naccuracy.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "English Speech Recognition\n\nAlthough both models perform within 0.1% of each other on LibriSpeech, a zero-shot Whisper model performs much better on other datasets than expected for its LibriSpeech performance and makes 55.2% less errors on average.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nMulti-lingual Speech Recognition and Translation\nWe find a strong squared correlation coefficient of 0.83 between the log of the word error rate and the log of the amount of training data per language. We also observed that many of the largest outliers in terms of worse than expected performance according to this trend are languages that have unique scripts and are more distantly related to the Indo-European languages making up the majority of the training dataset such as Hebrew (HE), Telugu (TE), Chinese (ZH), and Korean (KO). These differences could be due to a lack of transfer due to linguistic distance.\n\nFor translation the same correlation emerged, moreover since Whisper evaluation is zero-shot, it does particularly well on the lowest resource in CoVoST2\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nLanguage Identification\nThe zero-shot performance of Whisper is not competitive with prior supervised work here and underperforms the supervised SOTA by 13.6%.\n\nLong-form Transcription\nWe compare the performance with open-source models as well as 4 commercial ASR services. The results show that Whisper performs better than the compared models on most datasets\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nComparison with Human Performance\nTo quantify how close Whisper’s performance is to the human performance, they selected 25 recordings and compared transcripts produced by professional transcribers. These results\nindicate that Whisper’s English ASR performance is not perfect but very close to human-level accuracy.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
