    {
      "id": "mLeGFUyl_OAovSyD6R4Iy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -986.6876343034562,
      "y": 3578.5291566848755,
      "width": 934.6541748046875,
      "height": 1125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "XTmAyRMuPq8mJmIiFcs-3",
        "EA_tr5suNeOhWDvJz2_DF"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b366",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1199279444,
      "version": 1323,
      "versionNonce": 1172631803,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789416511819,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Training Data\n\nA detailed description of the training datasets is presented below.\n\n- People’s Speech: a large-scale English speech recognition dataset from the internet. The\ndataset includes interviews, radio and finance.\n- GigaSpeech: a multi-domain speech recognition corpus curated from audio-books, podcasts and\nYouTube. It contains both narrated and spontaneous speech over a range of content material,\nincluding science, arts and sports.\n- Common Voice: a crowd-source speech datasets where contributors record themselves\nnarrating text from Wikipedia in various languages.\n- Fisher: a corpus of two-sided conversational telephone calls amongst speakers from the\nUnited States.\n- LibriSpeech: a standard dataset for training and evaluating academic speech models. The\naudiobook domain provides high-quality recording conditions, with little to no background noise.\n- VoxPopuli is a large-scale multilingual speech datasets consisting of European\nParliament event recordings from 2009-2020.\n- TED-LIUM: a collection of English-language TED Talk conference videos. The talks span a\nvariety of cultural, political, and academic themes. \n- SwitchBoard: a corpus of two-sided conversational telephone calls amongst speakers from\nthe United States.\n- AMI: meeting recordings captured using multiple recording streams in parallel.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nExperimental Setup\n\nWe train for a total of 80,000 optimisation steps. They linearly increasing the learning rate\nfrom zero to a maximum of 1e-4 over the first 500 steps, and then linearly decaying it to zero.\nIf the student encoder is the same as the teacher encoder, we freeze its parameters and only\nrun it forward once.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Training Data\n\nA detailed description of the training datasets is presented below.\n\n- People’s Speech: a large-scale English speech recognition dataset from the internet. The dataset includes interviews, radio and finance.\n- GigaSpeech: a multi-domain speech recognition corpus curated from audio-books, podcasts and YouTube. It contains both narrated and spontaneous speech over a range of content material, including science, arts and sports.\n- Common Voice: a crowd-source speech datasets where contributors record themselves narrating text from Wikipedia in various languages.\n- Fisher: a corpus of two-sided conversational telephone calls amongst speakers from the United States.\n- LibriSpeech: a standard dataset for training and evaluating academic speech models. The audiobook domain provides high-quality recording conditions, with little to no background noise. \n- VoxPopuli is a large-scale multilingual speech datasets consisting of European\nParliament event recordings from 2009-2020.\n- TED-LIUM: a collection of English-language TED Talk conference videos. The talks span a variety of cultural, political, and academic themes. \n- SwitchBoard: a corpus of two-sided conversational telephone calls amongst speakers from the United States.\n- AMI: meeting recordings captured using multiple recording streams in parallel.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nExperimental Setup\n\nWe train for a total of 80,000 optimisation steps. They linearly increasing the learning rate from zero to a maximum of 1e-4 over the first 500 steps, and then linearly decaying it to zero. If the student encoder is the same as the teacher encoder, we freeze its parameters and only run it forward once.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
